      "S10"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Alan Sugar is a British entrepreneur who built his fortune through Amstrad's affordable electronics and home computers. Raised on a Hackney council estate, he started trading with his own savings. He later built the Amsprop commercial-property portfolio and became widely known as the investor at the centre of television's The Apprentice.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Before Amstrad had designed a satellite receiver, Alan Sugar committed to supplying equipment for Rupert Murdoch's planned television service. He promised a retail price of £199 and accepted the development risk without asking Murdoch to guarantee purchases. The commitment left his team eight months to prepare for Sky's launch.\n\nSugar and engineer Bob Watkins worked backwards from the target price. They negotiated components with GEC-Marconi, then looked beyond specialist dish suppliers to ordinary metalworking factories. A Midlands manufacturer bought coated steel directly from British Steel, pressed the dishes and packed them with their brackets. That arrangement delivered finished units ready for distribution, avoiding additional handling at Amstrad.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "His detailed published first-person account explicitly describes the promise, timetable, technical team and manufacturing arrangement."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S7"
    ],
    "word_count": 107,
    "html": "<p>Before Amstrad had designed a satellite receiver, Alan Sugar committed to supplying equipment for Rupert Murdoch's planned television service. He promised a retail price of £199 and accepted the development risk without asking Murdoch to guarantee purchases. The commitment left his team eight months to prepare for Sky's launch.</p>\r\n<p>Sugar and engineer Bob Watkins worked backwards from the target price. They negotiated components with GEC-Marconi, then looked beyond specialist dish suppliers to ordinary metalworking factories. A Midlands manufacturer bought coated steel directly from British Steel, pressed the dishes and packed them with their brackets. That arrangement delivered finished units ready for distribution, avoiding additional handling at Amstrad.</p>\r\n"
  },
